			<div class="content" id="post-content-4752740">
									

<p>Thanks for reading and commenting! And I totally see what you mean.</p>
<p>In my mind, our interaction with James is transitory. We don’t really know much of what he did during his year of travel, he was with us for a <em>long</em> time waiting for this escape idea, and we don’t know what’s on the other side of the wormhole for him. (They were only able to open it for 5 microseconds, so the Foundation hasn’t sent anything over to explore themselves yet. I was hoping that lent weight to James’ desperation for change from his situation.)</p>
<p>I didn’t want him locked up for eternity (especially when his eternity is so much longer than ours,) and I didn’t want to cure him, so this was my “give his story an ambiguous future plan.” I guess you only get so much set-up/payoff in a story this short 😂</p>

